<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-arginine degradation I (arginase pathway)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Ubiquitous Plant Pathways (UPP) that was generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most land plants. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Arginase-mediated |FRAME: ARG| degradation is widely distributed in the biosphere, and is found in all primary kingdoms |CITS: [8759304]|. The first step in this process, catalyzed by the enzyme arginase, is the hydrolysis of |FRAME:ARG| to form |FRAME:L-ORNITHINE| and |FRAME:UREA|. There are many variations of the arginase pathway, since the fate of the products of the arginase reaction may be different in different organisms. Variants of arginase-based |FRAME: ARG|-degradation pathways include |FRAME: ARGASEDEG-PWY|, |FRAME:ARG-PRO-PWY|, and |FRAME: ARG-GLU-PWY|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; This variant is the &quot;classical&quot; arginase pathway, having the widest distribution. The pathway is found in mammals as well as many bacteria. In this pathway |FRAME:ARG| is converted into |FRAME:GLT| in a series of three steps; following the initial step, catalyzed by arginase, |FRAME:L-ORNITHINE| is converted to |FRAME:L-GLUTAMATE_GAMMA-SEMIALDEHYDE| by |FRAME:CPLX-6747| (OAT). |FRAME:L-GLUTAMATE_GAMMA-SEMIALDEHYDE| undergoes a spontaneous conversion to |FRAME: L-DELTA1-PYRROLINE_5-CARBOXYLATE|, which is then converted in the third step, catalyzed by |FRAME:CPLX-6746|, to |FRAME:GLT|. In a subsequent step glutamate dehydrogenase (GDH) removes the amino group from |FRAME:GLT|, generating |FRAME:2-KETOGLUTARATE|, which is fed to the |FRAME:TCA| (see |FRAME: GLUTAMATE-DEG1-PWY|). Such conversion into |FRAME:GLT| is a common route for the degradation of amino acids, including |FRAME:GLN|, |FRAME:HIS|, and |FRAME:PRO| (see the pathways |FRAME: GLUTAMINDEG-PWY|, |FRAME: HISDEG-PWY| and |FRAME: PROUT-PWY|). In mammals, the enzymes catalyzing the reactions of this pathway are present in the mitochondrial matrix. There are two isoforms of mammalian arginases - a mitochondrial form, including the humane |FRAME:CPLX-6622|, and a cytosolic form, which includes the human |FRAME:CPLX-6621|. The cytosolic arginases, however, are believed to be involved mostly in the urea cycle (see |FRAME: PWY-4984|) |CITS: [6408859]|, although this notion has been questioned |CITS: [15050972]|. Human arginases were found in many tissues, including kidney and prostate, brain, skeletal muscle, placenta, lung, mammary gland, macrophage, uterus, testis, and gut |CITS: [9256072] [15050972]|. In prokaryotes, this pathway is used for the catabolism of |FRAME: ARG| as either a carbon or a nitrogen source. Arginases have been detected in and characterized from several species, including bacilii |CITS: [2029528][7540694]|, agrobacteria |CITS: [3758074][3957872]|, cyanobacteria |CITS: [Gupta81][100070][10648527]|, |FRAME:TAX-583| spp. |CITS: [4579440]|, |FRAME: TAX-271| |CITS: [64178]|, and mycobacteria |CITS: [13192096]|. However, in most of these cases the fate of |FRAME:L-ORNITHINE| is not known |CITS: [3534538]|. In some cases |FRAME: ARG| can can be utilized by the organism as both carbon and nitrogen source, while in others it is used only as a nitrogen source. In addition, some organisms possess the enzyme urease, which converts the urea formed by arginase into |FRAME: CARBON-MONOXIDE| and |FRAME: AMMONIA|, a favorite nitrogen source. In some strains, such as |FRAME: TAX-1402|, the pathway is subject to strong catabolite repression during growth on glucose |CITS: [5674049]|. In some strains, the pathway is also subject to nitrogen catabolite repression. Such regulation is species specific and is not universal |CITS: [6124533]|. Although this pathway was not originally believed to act in plants, later studies provided evidence for its existence |CITS: [18419821][18621980]|. For example, in |FRAME: TAX-3349| (Scots pine), this pathway appears to be required to provide the glutamate that is needed for glutamine biosynthesis during the early stages of seed germination |CITS: [18621980]|. However, further work will be required to determine if this is the only pathway that operates in plants, or whether additional pathways such as |FRAME: ARG-PRO-PWY| can also contribute to arginine degradation |CITS: [18419821][9576796]|.</Description>
